d to  be humbled every once in a while I  decided to move on to 1954 and wish I  have to admit mark for the first time  that I was slightly younger in 1964  Arthur's brilliance as a historian and  his brilliance as a writer  able to convey the sense of a  conversation as if the reader were a  vibrant part of it in right now the  here-and-now are in fulsol flower so in  this year he records one of the nation's  most shameful periods the faith he  describes in the American people should  hearten and fortify all of us in  troubled times today I arrived in  Chicago  Saturday evening September 12th after  registering at the Congress I went over  to the Conrad Hilton and located some  newspaperman sorry I'm going to change  this slide put my marker on the wrong  page once again President Truman came to  town today to address a fundraising  lunch for the Truman library I had 15  minutes to talk with him in the morning  in his suite at the Sheraton Plaza we  talked considerably about McCarthy he  then began to muse about the incidence  of periods of hysteria in American  history he told me as he had before that  he had completed a monograph on this  subject as he figures it the periodicity  is about eight to ten years thus from  the Alien and Sedition Acts to the trial  of Aaron Burr the know-nothing an anti  abolitionist sentiment to the 50s  reconstruction through the election of  1876 from a mitchell Palmer to the  campaign of 1928 so he guess is that it  will take McCarthyism eight to ten years  to burn itself out which means anywhere  from 1956 to 1960 before it is over but  he affirmed both touchingly and  impressively his face in the decency of  the American people and their capacity  to recover from these binges of fear and  panic HST looked very well trim natty  and cheerful as usual I want to read a  second passage because as the neighbor  and fell a member of Beekman place  association in which Arthur was active  my most vivid recollections linked him  to his  wonderful wife Alexandra I live in a  little maze net whose living room window  did double duty as a Vista on to one of  New York's most beautiful Blanc's and as  a portal onto an extraordinary couple  who watch together on our Street daily  sometimes many times daily Arthur's  admiration and affection for Alexandra  is reflected in his many references to  her in the journals including this  passage from 2000 on the 31st we went to  Washington to see the new millennium in  arriving in time for luncheon with  Robert I had been surprised to receive  the invitation for New Year's Eve at the  White House in the Clinton years  we have never been asked to a state  dinner we have dined twice at the White  House once at a farewell dinner for  Pamela Harriman on her way to Paris Pam  put our names on a guest list and once  that a dinner for Arts and Humanities  medalists as a medallist I had to be  invited both times though I was seated  next to Hillary alexandra thinks  probably correctly that we owe our  invitation this time to George Stevens  who is co-producing the ball any Alinta  tainment with Quincy Jones we stated the  hay-adams and made our way to the White  House around 6 o'clock a reporter asked  me how I would sum up the last  millennium I said I'm glad it's over  a long talk a hour follow during which  we were astonished by how few of the  other guests we even recognized I  suppose most were big contributors we  talked to Chris Dodd in his charming new  wife Jed and Vicky and Jean Kennedy the  Fred Dutton the Bob's runs a few others  at dinner Alexandre sitting between Alan  Greenspan chairman of the Federal  Reserve and Jill Wolfensohn president of  the World Bank held for a moment the  financial future of the world in her  hand Greenspan whom I have known  slightly for years has been much  humanized by his marriage to Andrea  Mitchell he came over and taught  cordially to me for some minutes and  Alexandra found him an engaging dinner  partner Arthur Schlesinger jr. was not  only an exceptional scholar but also as  we've heard an exceedingly generous  person on a number of occasions I  delivered armloads of his books to his  home with a request to autograph them  for New York Historical Society members  and friends he never complained about  minor venus which was extreme at times  and he always delivered the goods arthur  honored us several times with his  presence at evenings just like this one  he was always always interesting  he also honored us by invoking a speech  given at the New York Historical Society  in 1868 in his much read and much cited  article in the New York Review of Books  in April 2006 in that article he writes  that and I quote history is not  self-executing  you do not put a coin in the slot and  have history come out it's a funny way  of putting it but as we know very true  Arthur concludes that article with a few  lines that are worth adding I think to  our Vedas this evening I quote this is  the endless fascination of historical  writing the search to reconstruct what  went before a quest illuminated by those  ever-changing prisms that can  can you replace old questions in a new  light as a great Dutch historian Peter  Gayle was fond of saying history is  indeed an argument without end that I  believe is why we love it so much we do  love history and we did love Arthur  Schlessinger jr. when mrs. intelligence  his wit and his friendship thank you  we're open to some questions for the  members of the panel from the audience  it's a it's a large book but I imagine a  lot has been edited in the preparation  of the journal is there any insight from  family members as to what and why mate  something was edited or not included  basically it was a Arizona from the  greater than 6,000 pages we had to cut  out around the 4/5 or 3/4 and an other  volume could be spun out of this but and  maybe they will but we concentrated on  the everything related to his political  life and political views and American  politics so there you go well we since  there are representatives of penguin  here maybe we can ask them if they feel  like publishing the other 5 volumes that  have not yet appeared in print I hope  they will  in the context of the dis uniting in the  view of America which is obviously a  diatribe against multiculturalism what  was the negative response if any in the  mail that showed up at the household  mail did show up in the house that who  here is pretty oh I'm sorry I'm assuming  there was a strong negative response  from any number of people and I was  wondering what it was yeah you know  there was the diss uniting of America  was one of our there was a great  columnist as well as scholar and when  some of his writing is very measured and  some of it is highly polemical does this  uniting view of America which was a book  published in the 90s I can't remember  exactly what year was as this gentleman  pointed out a attack on what was then  called multiculturalism and its impact  on the writing of history and on  intellectual life in general which he  thought as the title suggests was  fragmenting the society and fragmenting  intellectual processes and purposes and  I think it did there were a lot of  people who were very who took a very  different point of view very vocally and  there was a lot of debate in the  historical profession about whether this  critique was fully warranted etc what's  interesting is that later on I myself  heard ought to say that he actually  regretted a little bit the tone of that  book that he felt he had failed to sort  of indicate a kind of common ground that  some of the some of the writers he was  criticizing might actually have with  some of his earlier writing and that and  that he realized that there was another  danger to intellectual life coming from  the opposite side of the political  spectrum of people trying to kind of  create a switch you might call a  patriotically correct history which left  no room for all the diversity of  American society but there was you know  it was controversial but one thing that  I could say about authors he doesn't shy  from controversy  things as he saw them you know on the  October 15 event that Eric and I are  going to be at at the Graduate Center  David levering Lewis the extraordinary  historian is one of the people who's  taking part and I asked everyone to talk  about a particular book or passage from  a book that affected them and that  contributed to their better  understanding of what was going on  David levering Lewis is going to speak  on chose the dis uniting of of America  as you come to come to the session Neil  here my father goes into the destruction  of that in the diaries so you'll find  that quite interesting but one of his  comments was he wasn't sure he suddenly  for the first time in his life a lot of  conservatives were supporting his point  of view and he wanted her to see had  done you know what that was all about so  you can read a lot them I would like to  comment briefly on Eric Foner's reading  of the passages relating to Adlai  Stevenson on November 7th there'll be a  meeting here at the Historical Society  about Adlai Stevenson at five o'clock in  the afternoon and that will be a good  occasion that perhaps put it more  perspective well 10 or whatever those  comments reflected as to the caution  that conservative that Stevenson  reflected in democratic politics in 1952  I think the I think Arthur felt deeply  that no man who ever ran for the  presidency and wasn't elected had more  influence on the democratic processes in  our country than Adlai Stevenson did and  had greatly and profoundly influenced  the whole generation who then came to  power  later it and he very reluctantly not  reluctantly he made a very determined  judgment that Kennedy was a stronger  political candidate and he was not sure  that Kennedy was any more liberal than  certainly than Stevenson was but the  thing that I think held the Stephens  onions